\details{
A spelling that could be pronounced more than one way gets \strong{more than
one code}, which is the point of the algorithm: \code{"CH"} may be a 4
(as in \code{"church"}) or a 5 (as in \code{"loch"}), so \code{"Auerbach"} codes as
both \code{097400} and \code{097500}. To use the codes for blocking, treat each
one as a pass of its own: two records are candidates when any of their
codes agree. \code{simplify = TRUE} returns the codes as a matrix, which
\code{\link[=block_pairs]{block_pairs()}} takes as one blocking pass per column.

Before coding, letters are lower-cased, the accented Latin letters the
rules list are folded (\code{"ß"} to \code{"s"}, \code{"é"} to \code{"e"}, \code{"ł"} to \code{"l"}),
and everything else that is not a letter of the algorithm's alphabet --
digits, punctuation, spaces, and letters of other scripts -- is dropped.
So \code{"Jackson-Jackson"}, \code{"Jackson Jackson"} and \code{"JacksonJackson"} all
